<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Hats and Shoes! – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> All About My Body</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Hats and Shoes!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow two-step spoken instructions related to classroom activities. Interactive: Respond in a predictable pattern to simple questions about familiar things.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow two-step instructions related to body movements (e.g., "Raise your hand and wave." or "Touch your nose and clap your hands."). Can listen and respond to predictable patterns presented in rhymes, songs, and chants (e.g., "Put your left foot in, take your left foot out."). Can follow and answer questions about body parts using gestures or short answers (e.g., "Where is your hat?" "On my head.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, "Hello, everyone!" and starts a "Simon Says" game using familiar body parts. "Simon says, touch your head!" "Simon says, touch your foot!" The teacher then shows realia (a hat, a pair of shoes) and flashcards for "hat" and "shoes," asking "What's this?" Students point or say single words. The teacher then quickly introduces new flashcards for 'hand', 'nose', 'ear', 'mouth', saying the words clearly while pointing to their own body part.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of new vocabulary with clear articulation and gestures, using flashcards and pointing to body parts. "This is my <b>h</b>ead." (points to head). "This is my <b>h</b>and." (points to hand). "This is a <b>h</b>at." (shows hat). The teacher emphasizes the /h/ sound in "hat," "hand," "head," and the /t/ sound in "toes" (if introduced later in a song) by slightly exaggerating the sound. The teacher also models imperative sentences: "Touch your head." "Put on your hat." (demonstrating with realia).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using simple questions and examples.
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a shoe flashcard) "Is this a hat?" (Expected answer: No!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to their own hand) "Is this my nose?" (Expected answer: No! or "Hand!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to a student's ear and asks) "What's this?" (Expected answer: Ear! or students point to their own ear).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input:</b> The teacher presents a simple, repetitive song or chant about body parts and putting on clothes (e.g., a simplified "Head, Shoulders, Knees, and Toes" or "This is the way we put on our shoes"). The song will incorporate target vocabulary: 'head', 'hand', 'foot', 'nose', 'ear', 'mouth', 'hat', 'shoes', 'socks'. The teacher plays the audio or sings/chants clearly while performing actions for each part and clothing item.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Audio recording of a simple song/chant (e.g., "My Body, My Clothes Song") or teacher-led chant, accompanied by large, colorful flashcards of body parts (head, hand, foot, nose, ear, mouth) and clothing items (hat, shoes, socks).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listen and follow predictable patterns presented in rhymes, songs, and chants; identify body parts and clothing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Point:</b> As the song plays, students listen and point to the corresponding body part or flashcard that the teacher shows.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Act (TPR):</b> Students listen and perform simple actions from the song (e.g., "touch your head," "clap your hands").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Circle/Tap:</b> The teacher says a word (e.g., "hat"). Students listen and tap the correct flashcard or object on their desk.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening to and following one- and two-step spoken instructions using imperatives ("Touch your head," "Put on your hat"). Identifying body parts (head, hand, foot, nose, ear, mouth) and clothing (hat, shoes, socks). Understanding WH-questions ("Where is your hat?"). Pronunciation practice of initial /h/ and /t/ sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listen and Do (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher gives a series of one-step and simple two-step instructions. Students listen and perform the actions. "Touch your nose." "Raise your hand." "Touch your head and clap your hands." "Put on your hat" (students pretend or use realia). The teacher closely monitors and corrects as needed.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Listen and Identify (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher holds up flashcards of body parts and clothing. The teacher asks "Where is your hat?" (students point to their head or a picture of a hat). "This is my _________." (pointing to a foot, students listen and complete "foot" or point). The teacher models the initial sounds, e.g., "<b>H</b>at. Listen: /h/." Students listen and repeat chorally.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Listen, Arrange, and Check (Pair Practice):</b> Students work in pairs with sets of picture cards (one set of body parts, one set of clothing items). The teacher gives instructions like: "Put the hat on the head picture." "Put the socks next to the shoes picture." Students listen and arrange their cards. After each instruction, partners briefly check each other's arrangement, reinforcing listening comprehension.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> "Dress the Paper Person!" (Listening Game). Students receive a cutout paper person and small paper cutouts of clothing items (hat, shoes, socks) and body parts (optional, to place on the person). The activity is designed to have students actively listen and follow two-step instructions in a realistic, playful scenario.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Description:</b> The teacher gives clear two-step instructions using imperative verbs. Students listen carefully and place the clothing items on their paper person or perform body actions. For instance: "First, put the socks on the foot, then put on the shoes." or "Put the hat on the head, and touch your nose." The teacher monitors closely, ensuring students are listening and executing the instructions correctly. This focuses on functional use of target vocabulary and instructions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> Directly uses "Hats and Shoes!" and "All About My Body" vocabulary and the function of following directions to dress a figure.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in following two-step spoken commands, collaborative interaction (if students help each other slightly), and successful completion of the listening task. Students show their "dressed" paper person to the teacher/class.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist for individual student comprehension and ability to follow instructions. Quick review of a "Listen and Draw" activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> "Listen and Draw/Color." Students are given a simple drawing template of a person (a stick figure or outline) on paper. The teacher gives a series of instructions for students to follow. Example instructions: "Draw a hat on the head." "Color the shoes red." "Draw a happy mouth." "Now, touch your ear and then draw a nose." The teacher collects and quickly reviews the drawings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to follow one- and two-step spoken instructions (e.g., drawing correct items in correct places, performing physical actions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identification of target vocabulary (head, hand, foot, nose, ear, mouth, hat, shoes, socks) through drawing/action.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responding to simple WH-questions (e.g., "Where is your hat?").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are asked two simple reflective prompts:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you understand the instructions today?" (Students show thumbs up/down or green/red card).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you touch your hat and then your nose?" (Students perform the action).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new English words about our body or clothes did you hear and understand today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to listen to two instructions and do them?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson to enhance listening skills for 2nd graders? (e.g., TPR, song, visual aids, clear instructions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with two-step instructions or vocabulary recognition? (e.g., more repetition, slower pace, simpler instructions, individual visual prompts).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The vocabulary (head, hand, foot, nose, ear, mouth, hat, shoes, socks) and basic phrases ("This is my...", "Put on your...") introduced and practiced through listening will be reinforced in Lesson 2, which will focus on reading comprehension of short texts or labels featuring these same words and concepts.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>